  "worked_solution": "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(\\frac{17\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{340}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $-\\frac{17\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to $-{340}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and a vertex that lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222483/assets/a82ca9d59a88071320c95a78d10900c73c32e4c156dddb32a57603121923b8c5.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
